    "https://careers.utah.edu/employers/post-job-or-internship/": {
      "title": "U Career Success",
      "description": "Post a Job or Internship – U Career Success Skip to content U CAREER SUCCESS MENU Students Career Coaches Internships+ Events International Student Resources Career Courses Resources and Tools Employers Employers Home Post a Job or Internship Starting an Internship Program Next-Level Engagement Career Fair Recruiting Campus Partners Campus Partners Home Workshops Co-Sponsor an Event U Career Success Leadership More Career Impact Awards Contact Us Search Site Powered by the University of Utah Powered by Search Campus U Career Success Career Coaches Recruiters & Employers Campus Partners Events Home Recruiters & Employers Post a Job or Internship 40,000+ U Students are on Handshake LOG IN TO HANDSHAKE Post Jobs and Internships on Handshake for Maximum Visibility Handshake is the premiere hub for connecting with students who are actively refining the skills you need in your workforce. Ready to post your job or internship? LOG IN TO HANDSHAKE Note: Job and internship postings are subject to U of U recruiting policies. New to recruiting at the U? Email us at [email protected] to get matched with a dedicated Relationship Manager . Get Started With Handshake 1. Create an Account for Free 2. Post a Job or Internship 3. Allow 2-3 Business Days for Review and Approval Join Handshake Handshake Support Articles Getting Started with Handshake: Employers How to Post a Job Confirming Your Job is Posted Successfully Create a High Quality Employer Profile Ready to Start an Internship Program? Starting your internship program could be easier than you think. And it can benefit your organization for years to come. Learn How To Get Started Students Log in to Handshake Events Employers Employers Home Post a Job or Internship Next-Level Engagement Career Fairs & Expos Recruiting Policies U Career Success 1731 E Campus Center Dr, STE 1300 SALT LAKE CITY, UT 84112 801-587-8687 Top Resources International Student Resources Other Campus Partners Contact Us © 2026 THE UNIVERSITY OF UTAH Nondiscrimination & Accessibility Disclaimer Privacy About the U Media Contacts"
    },
    "https://careers.utah.edu/employers/internships/": {
      "title": "U Career Success",
      "description": "Starting an Internship Program – U Career Success Skip to content U CAREER SUCCESS MENU Students Career Coaches Internships+ Events International Student Resources Career Courses Resources and Tools Employers Employers Home Post a Job or Internship Starting an Internship Program Next-Level Engagement Career Fair Recruiting Campus Partners Campus Partners Home Workshops Co-Sponsor an Event U Career Success Leadership More Career Impact Awards Contact Us Search Site Powered by the University of Utah Powered by Search Campus U Career Success Career Coaches Recruiters & Employers Campus Partners Events Home Recruiters & Employers Starting an Internship Program Start an Internship Program Request Internship Playbook Why Build an Internship Program? Develop a talent pipeline for future hires Bring fresh ideas and innovation into your organization Provide mentorship opportunities for current employees Increase your company’s visibility and employer brand Support time-consuming or high-volume tasks Internship Guidelines Duration: About 10 weeks, with defined start and end dates Hours worked: 10-20 hours a week during the academic term, up to 40 during the summer. Pay: Interns should be paid regardless of whether they earn academic credit for the demonstrated learning associated with their internship. Pay rate amount depends on industry. All Internships Should Include A learning experience that gives students the opportunity to gain valuable, real-world experience. Clearly defined start and end dates Clearly defined goals or learning objectives that meet the university's requirements. Direct supervision by a professional who can provide productive, feedback, guidance, and resources. A position with clear responsibilities and required qualifications. Get The Ultimate Internship Playbook Our detailed Internship Playbook walks you through deciding what type of internship program to build and the steps to creating a successful program. It also includes examples of internship projects, role descriptions, offer letters, and more! Reach out to your Relationship Manager or email [email protected] for a copy! Request the Playbook How do I get started? Do I have to pay my intern? Can I offer credit instead of pay? How much should I pay my intern? How many hours should I require? How do I find interns? Establish your goals for the internship program and identify a meaningful project or role that is important to your organization that can be done by an intern. Determine how much support your program will need from the organization and find a manager who can help with onboarding, supervision, feedback, and performance evaluations. Sort out logistics for equipment and software needed, pay, workspace, and other details relevant to the role or project. Create a job description that includes: What interns can expect to learn Approximate start and end dates Desired hours per week Location Compensation Work with your relationship manager to reach students who would be a good fit. Build an onboarding plan. We expect internships to be paid. Paying interns increases your hiring pool, provides financial support for students, and encourages loyalty, investment, and accountability. Read NACE’s position statement on unpaid internships . Unpaid internship job listings in Handshake are approved on a case-by-case basis. No. Decisions regarding the awarding of academic credit are solely within the purview of the University of Utah faculty. Hourly wages for interns can vary widely depending on the industry, skillset of the student, and their level of experience. An hourly wage cannot be less than the minimum wage for the state in which the student will be working. Internships are typically part-time during fall and spring semesters, adding up to between 10-20 hours a week; not to exceed 20 hours a week (August-May). Summer internships, or those during a semester in which the student is not enrolled in courses, can require up to 40 hours a week. We strongly encourage internships that require more than 20 hours/week of students to be paid on an hourly or stipend basis, regardless of industry. Handshake is the primary place for employers to find applicants for internships and jobs. Additional recruiting options are available here . New to Recruiting at the U? Email us at [email protected] to get matched with a dedicated Relationship Manager. Students Log in to Handshake Events Employers Employers Home Post a Job or Internship Next-Level Engagement Career Fairs & Expos Recruiting Policies U Career Success 1731 E Campus Center Dr, STE 1300 SALT LAKE CITY, UT 84112 801-587-8687 Top Resources International Student Resources Other Campus Partners Contact Us © 2026 THE UNIVERSITY OF UTAH Nondiscrimination & Accessibility Disclaimer Privacy About the U Media Contacts"
    },
    "https://careers.utah.edu/employers/next-level-engagement/": {
      "title": "U Career Success",
      "description": "Next-Level Engagement – U Career Success Skip to content U CAREER SUCCESS MENU Students Career Coaches Internships+ Events International Student Resources Career Courses Resources and Tools Employers Employers Home Post a Job or Internship Starting an Internship Program Next-Level Engagement Career Fair Recruiting Campus Partners Campus Partners Home Workshops Co-Sponsor an Event U Career Success Leadership More Career Impact Awards Contact Us Search Site Powered by the University of Utah Powered by Search Campus U Career Success Career Coaches Recruiters & Employers Campus Partners Events Home Recruiters & Employers Next-Level Engagement Next-Level Engagement Connect With Students There are many opportunities to engage with students on campus. Work with your Relationship Manager to set something up. On-Campus Engagement Ideas Tabling: Set up a table on campus to chat with students about your company and industry. Presentations: Schedule info sessions, panels, and workshops where students can learn more about your company or your industry. Student Organizations: Schedule something with a specific club or organization for the opportunity to connect with active students who are interested in your industry. Targeted Marketing: Work with U Career Success to market to students on campus through digital and print materials. We can help you market events as well as specific opportunities. Sponsorship: Make a strong impression and a lasting impact by sponsoring a project, case competition, event, or student activity. New to Recruiting at the U? Email us at [email protected] to get matched with a dedicated Relationship Manager. Students Log in to Handshake Events Employers Employers Home Post a Job or Internship Next-Level Engagement Career Fairs & Expos Recruiting Policies U Career Success 1731 E Campus Center Dr, STE 1300 SALT LAKE CITY, UT 84112 801-587-8687 Top Resources International Student Resources Other Campus Partners Contact Us © 2026 THE UNIVERSITY OF UTAH Nondiscrimination & Accessibility Disclaimer Privacy About the U Media Contacts"
    },
